WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
The inverted index.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.992
A book’s index maps words to page numbers.

3
00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:13.979
An inverted index does the same for a corpus: for each term, a postings list of the documents that contain it, usually with the count of occurrences in each.

4
00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:21.747
To answer a query you look up each query term, take the union of the postings lists as candidates, and score only those.

5
00:00:22.027 --> 00:00:30.859
A corpus of ten million chunks may have a few thousand candidates for a specific query, which is why keyword search is fast without any GPUs.

6
00:00:31.139 --> 00:00:41.208
Three statistics fall out of the index, and every classic ranking function uses them: Term frequency, tf: how many times the term occurs in this document.

7
00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:45.285
Document frequency, n: how many documents contain the term.

8
00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:47.464
Rare terms are more informative.

9
00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:52.096
Document length, dl, and the average length avgdl across the corpus.

10
00:00:52.376 --> 00:00:56.899
Before any of that comes tokenization: the rule that turns text into terms.

11
00:00:57.179 --> 00:00:59.099
This is where most real bugs live.

12
00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:16.616
A tokenizer that lowercases and splits on every non-letter turns “TS-999” into “ts” and “999”, and now a query for TS-999 also matches every document that mentions 999 users.

13
00:01:16.896 --> 00:01:23.232
Stemming (“renewals” to “renewal”), stop-word removal, and handling of accents all change what matches.

14
00:01:23.512 --> 00:01:26.115
None of these choices is universally right.

15
00:01:26.395 --> 00:01:30.875
Pick them for your corpus, and test them with the queries your users actually type.
